875, -0.0120086669921875, 0.0157928466796875, 0.025054931640625, 0.02764892578125, -0.01212310791015625, -0.02166748046875, -0.0010461807250976562, 0.022735595703125, 0.007526397705078125, -0.0106353759765625, 0.047515869140625, -0.0294342041015625, 0.0099029541015625, 0.0011892318725585938, 0.0193939208984375, 0.0228271484375, 0.019989013671875, 0.0233001708984375, -0.00860595703125, 0.036651611328125, -0.003082275390625, 0.03155517578125, -0.0052490234375, 0.005970001220703125, -0.00543975830078125, -0.01186370849609375, -0.00260162353515625, 0.006343841552734375, 0.00722503662109375, 0.0010251998901367188, 0.027587890625, 0.018890380859375, -0.0123443603515625, -0.0186920166015625, 0.0063934326171875, -0.01702880859375, 0.0181427001953125, 0.044677734375, 0.01287078857421875, 0.0006451606750488281, -0.01503753662109375, 0.0010900497436523438, 0.007659912109375, -0.01485443115234375, -0.03607177734375, 0.0086517333984375, -0.016845703125, 0.0007967948913574219, -0.002288818359375, -0.0080718994140625, 0.0089874267578125, 0.003536224365234375, -0.0181884765625, -0.0156402587890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "---\n**Introduction**\n\nRecent advances in large-scale image generation have largely been enabled by improvements in the quality and scalability of multimodal datasets. However, current dataset construction pipelines\u2014such as those used for LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022)\u2014tend to optimize for individual, task-specific domains in isolation. This siloed approach limits the ability to systematically transfer supervision across related generative tasks, reduces data efficiency, and complicates progress on unified multimodal capabilities. Here, we introduce a modular, capability-driven dataset design and curation protocol that enables cross-capability transfer, more robust performance, and systematic bias mitigation.\n\n---\n\n**Background, Definitions, and Motivation**\n\nA unified and systematic structure for multimodal data curation is needed to drive more holistic generative modeling. We define and clarify foundational terms as follows:\n\n- **Capability:** A discrete, functional generative skill that a model is expected to learn\u2014examples include text-to-image pairing, image editing, or linking images to entities. Each capability is bounded by its own task-specific objectives and supervision signals.\n- **Engine:** A dedicated and modular annotation or data pipeline crafted to generate training data for exactly one capability. Engines operate fully independently, ensuring that annotation schemas and supervision fields are strictly separated, thereby minimizing overlap or leakage between capabilities.\n- **Scheduler:** A meta-system designed to analyze empirical dependencies among capabilities and orchestrate the order in which data or supervision from various engines is introduced into model training. The scheduler relies on measured improvements and prerequisite relations (see Curriculum Scheduling section).\n\nRecent developments in multimodal generation leverage web-scale data, but often treat text-to-image synthesis, image editing, and knowledge association as isolated pursuits. Our approach instead structures data curation around these well-separated engines under the coordination of a scheduler to promote transfer, supervision sharing, and unified progress.\n\n---\n\n**Summary of Dataset and Contribution Overview**\n\nTo address the inefficiencies of capability isolation, we present a dataset organized explicitly along three primary capabilities. Table A summarizes coverage:\n\n| Capability                     | Example                   | Instances (M) | % of Total | Key Subgroup Coverage      |\n|------------------------------- |--------------------------|---------------|------------|----------------------------|\n| Text-image grounding           | \"a red fire truck\"        |     440       | 75.0%      | FairFace, gender, objects  |\n| Inter-image transformation     | \"day to night edit\"       |     120       | 20.5%      | Age, weather, context      |\n| Image-knowledge association    | cityscape \u2192 Q2364         |      27       | 4.5%       | Geographic, rare entities  |\n\n> **Dataset filtering and composition:**  \nAfter more than 70M low-quality or inappropriate candidate items were removed (criteria include inappropriateness, duplication, and model-based low-confidence scoring), the resulting dataset contains approximately 587M unique instances. Each key subgroup (e.g., FairFace categories) is balanced through enforced minimum representation of 5% within its capability subset. All validation metrics are based on non-overlapping random splits; detailed split sizes are provided in subsequent sections.\n\n---\n\n**Section Transition:**  \nBelow we detail the methodology used for data curation, quality assurance, and training infrastructure, followed by the analysis of scheduling, bias mitigation, and outcome summaries.\n\n---\n\n**Methodology: Data Curation and Infrastructure**\n\nTo support effective supervision transfer and prevent annotation confounds, our **capability-driven infrastructure** organizes three fully separated engines. A schematic is provided in Figure 1; a central summary of QA statistics and error sources is given in Table B below.\n\n**1. Text-image grounding engine:**\n- **Task:** Aligns concise text prompts with semantically matched images. Knowledge/entity linking and edit metadata are excluded by design, with strict separation of annotation fields.\n- **Sources:** Automated captioning (BLIP, CLIP, domain experts), plus expert annotation.\n- **QA Protocol:** For every 100-caption batch, at least 5 double-blind items are reviewed. Across 5,500 reviewed items, inter-annotator agreement Cohen\u2019s \u03ba = 0.89\u20130.93.\n- **Common errors:** Lexical ambiguity (56%), inadequate specificity (22%), overlapping entity terms (11%).\n\n**2. Inter-image transformation engine:**\n- **Task:** Supervises image edit understanding with labeled before/after image pairs and explicit transformation tags.\n- **Sources:** Curated benchmarks (e.g., CelebA-HQ Edits), CycleGAN augmentations, user-verified edits.\n- **QA Protocol:** All ambiguous cases double-blind reviewed (n = 8,600, \u03ba = 0.92). Edit saliency validated both by ResNet-50 classifiers (ROC-AUC: 0.94, 95% CI [0.938, 0.945], n = 10,000 pairs) and annotators (>90% correct on n = 4,200 items).\n- **Common errors:** Subtle global edits (44%), class leakage (17%), local effect misapplication (13%).\n\n**3. Image-knowledge association engine:**\n- **Task:** Maps images to discrete knowledge entities (e.g., cityscape \u2192 Wikidata Q2364) using entity-linking tools (BLINK, TagMe).\n- **QA Protocol:** Low-certainty/ambiguous mappings are escalated to panels of annotators (n = 7,200 cases; majority vote).  \n- **Agreement:** \u03ba = 0.87; errors mainly include confusion between visually similar entities or rare classes (<0.5%).\n\n**Centralized QA/Error Summary (Table B):**\n\n| Engine         | Inter-Annotator Agreement (\u03ba) | Main Error Types                 | Error Prevalence (%)    |\n|----------------|------------------------------|----------------------------------|------------------------|\n| Grounding      | 0.89\u20130.93                    | Lexical ambiguity, low specificity, entity overlap | 6\u201310                  |\n| Transformation | 0.92                         | Subtle edits, class leakage, local effects | 8\u201311            |\n| Knowledge      | 0.87                         | Similar entities, rare classes   | 3\u20136                   |\n\n**Operational Separation:**  \nAnnotation schema isolation is automated and enforced. Cross-engine annotation overlap remains below 0.04% (from 500k pair audits), with contaminated samples discarded. Controlled task-overlap is \u22640.1% (for benchmarking, provenance-tagged), and error impact assessed via explicit ablations. Edge cases are double-blind reviewed, and all field overlaps are systematically scanned.\n\n---\n\n**Curriculum Scheduling and Task Dependency Construction**\n\nThe **curriculum scheduler** automates training order by leveraging an empirically built dependency graph among capabilities:\n\n- **Dependency identification algorithm:** For each capability, we train ablation models excluding supervision from that capability, then add it back and measure gain in downstream performance (zero-shot accuracy, edit classification, entity precision). These supervised ablations (10M per major capability, smaller for entity) are repeated on three random splits.\n    - Dependencies are inferred when adding a capability results in a statistically significant and stable improvement (p < 0.01, paired t-test) in downstream metrics.\n    - For real-world interpretation, a 3.5% absolute boost in edit classification accuracy (from 59.6% to 63.1%, 2M edits, 95% CI [2.9, 4.0]) after including text-image grounding is substantial\u2014comparable to improvements gained by doubling annotation depth in prior SOTA benchmarks.\n    - Metrics tracked: zero-shot CLIP Top-1/5 accuracy, FID (over 50k samples), edit accuracy, entity micro-precision (all over 2M held-out items).\n\n- **Scheduler automation:** The scheduler iteratively updates the dependency graph, introducing capabilities that compensate for rising validation error. Underperforming or missing capabilities (e.g., entity supervision) reliably degrade related downstream accuracy (see Experimental Design for ablation effect sizes).\n- **Coordination with engines:** Scheduler decisions are logged, and capability additions are gated by QA-passed data only.\n\n---\n\n**Bias Mitigation Protocols and Empirical Outcomes**\n\nThe dataset curation pipeline robustly mitigates representational and outcome bias, but some gaps remain. The protocol has three phases:\n\n1. **Subgroup Representation:** Drawing on FairFace and Balanced ImageNet, every batch and capability is tracked for key subgroups; minimum subgroup representation is \u22655% or the batch median. Monitored subgroups are ethnicity, gender, age (grounding/edit), and geography/rarity (entity).\n\n2. **Thresholding, Rebalancing, and Synthetic Augmentation:** When a subgroup falls >2 SD below the mean, we increase representation using upsampling and StyleGAN2-based synthesis. An example: representation for underrepresented ethnicities rose from 2.9% (\u00b10.4%) to 7.1% (\u00b10.5%) after intervention, out of 80M samples. Additional compositional and attribute swaps are used for enrichment, and distributions are tested for parity (KS test, p > 0.4).\n\n3. **Audit and Reporting (Table 3 summary):**\n   - **Quantitative outcomes:** Mean F1 for underrepresented groups improved by up to 8.0 points; class entropy increased from 0.65 to 0.79; entity ratios rose by 11%. For practical context, these improvements reduce real-world failure modes, e.g., false negative rates in minority attributes, by more than a third.\n   - **Qualitative caveats:** Largest gaps persist for rare ethnicities or fine-grained occupational cues, as evidenced in Table 3. For example, entity classification remains <70% accurate for visually similar rare professions lacking training examples, and synthesized samples for some underrepresented groups have been flagged by auditors as occasionally artifact-prone or ambiguous.\n   - **Limitation:** While interventions markedly improve parity for major"}}